Short:        Two-way Nextcloud folder sync
Author:       Axel Hubinette <axel.hubinette@gmail.com>
Uploader:     Axel Hubinette <axel.hubinette@gmail.com>
Type:         comm/tcp
Version:      1.1
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 3.1.0
Requires:     AmiSSL v5 (util/libs/AmiSSL-v5-OS3.lha), TCP/IP stack, 68020


NextSync keeps folders on your Amiga in step with folders on a Nextcloud
server, over HTTPS with real certificate checking. It is about 49K, has a
Workbench interface and a CLI in the same binary, and needs nothing beyond
AmiSSL and a TCP/IP stack -- the interface uses gadtools.library, which has
been in ROM since Kickstart 2.0, so there is no GUI toolkit to install.

Written for the A1200 and A4000. Tested on a 68020 without an FPU and on a
68040.


WHAT IT DOES

  Two way sync. Changes on either side are carried to the other: new
  files, changed files, new drawers, deletions. Folders are followed all
  the way down, not just their top level.

  Sensible change detection. The server's etag and the local size and
  modification time are recorded after every transfer, so a second run
  transfers nothing, and a run after you edit one file transfers exactly
  that file. This survives a reboot.

  Conflicts never lose data. If a file changed on both sides, the local
  copy is renamed to <name>.conflict and the server copy is downloaded,
  so both survive and you can decide.

  Deletions are safe on first contact. A deletion is only carried across
  for a file that is on record as having been synced before, so pointing
  NextSync at a drawer that already has things in it will not empty it.

  Streaming transfers. 16K blocks, so a file never has to fit in RAM.
  Downloads are written to <name>.nspart and only renamed into place when
  complete and the length matches what the server said, so an interrupted
  transfer cannot leave a file that looks finished.

  TLS 1.3 through AmiSSL v5, checked against the AmiSSL certificate store.

  Local file dates are preserved on the server (X-OC-MTime).


SETTING IT UP

  Start it with no configuration and the Preferences window opens by
  itself; afterwards it is under Project. Fill in the server, your user
  name and your password, and press Connect. NextSync opens the
  connection there and then and tells you if it cannot, and on success
  the box below fills with the folders in your account. Click the ones
  you want synced, Browse for the drawer to keep them in, and Save.

  The password is not shown while you type it, but it is stored in plain
  text in NextSync.conf: AmigaOS has nowhere to keep a secret, and the
  password has to be presented to the server on every request. Use a
  Nextcloud app password (Settings, Security on the server) rather than
  your account password -- it can be revoked on its own, without touching
  the rest of your account.


USING IT

  NextSync            open the window
  NextSync SYNC       sync everything and exit, for scripts and startups
  NextSync LIST [dir] list a directory on the server
  NextSync PREFS      open Preferences on its own

  A sync in progress can be stopped: the Sync now button reads Stop sync
  while one is running, as do ESC and the close gadget, and CTRL-C does
  it from the CLI. It stops after the file being transferred finishes, so
  nothing is left half written, and the next run carries on from there.


REQUIREMENTS

  AmigaOS 3.1 or newer, 68020 or better    AmiSSL v5 needs a 68020
  About 6 MB of free RAM                   OpenSSL 3.x is not small
  A TCP/IP stack                           Roadshow, AmiTCP, Miami --
                                           anything with bsdsocket.library
  AmiSSL 5.x                               util/libs/AmiSSL-v5-OS3.lha
  mathieeedoubbas.library and              both ship with Workbench;
  mathieeedoubtrans.library in LIBS:       AmiSSL will not start without
                                           them


KNOWN LIMITS

  File names with a ':' in them cannot exist on an Amiga filesystem and
  are skipped with a line in the log. Length limits are left to the
  filesystem: FFS stops at 30 characters, PFS3 and SFS allow about 100.

  UTF-8 names pass through as bytes. Names with non-ASCII characters look
  wrong in Workbench but go back to the server unchanged.

  Times are treated as UTC on both sides. Set the Amiga clock to UTC or
  live with a constant offset; either way the first sync settles it.

  One connection and one transfer at a time. This is a 68020.

  Nextcloud's chunked upload is not implemented, so a very large upload
  depends on the server's request timeout.

  A file edited in place without its size or timestamp changing is not
  noticed. Catching that means hashing every file on every run, which
  costs more than it is worth on this hardware.


INSTALLING

  Install AmiSSL 5.x with its own installer first, then copy the NextSync
  drawer wherever you like. There is nothing else to set up: the
  configuration file is written next to the program by the Preferences
  window.

  Full documentation is in NextSync.guide.


SOURCE

  https://github.com/amiwoodcarver/nextsync-amiga

  Written in C for m68k-amigaos-gcc. The repository has the build
  environment, the emulator test rig and notes on the AmigaOS specifics
  that cost real time along the way -- AmiSSL's startup requirements, the
  amiga.lib sprintf trap, and how a password gadget is done with a string
  edit hook.

  Bug reports and patches are welcome.


THANKS

  To Jens Maus and everyone behind AmiSSL, without which none of this
  would be possible on a 68k Amiga in 2026.
